# id: BM63-write-apply-outside-the-lock
# blinds: a Write choosing its WAL and its memtable under ONE lock acquisition
# covering-lane: cpp-tsan
# expect: killed
# control-lane: cpp-build
# expect-control: alive
#
# B1's shape, restored -- and it was correct in B1, because nothing ever
# replaced the WAL or the memtable. The flush replaces BOTH, so in the gap
# between naming the WAL and applying to the memtable a Write can put its record
# in a log about to be deleted and its data in a memtable the flushed table does
# not contain. A LOST WRITE, with no corruption anywhere and nothing afterwards
# to see.
#
# COVERED BY cpp-tsan, because a lost write is what the race produces and the
# race is what a sanitizer can observe. The harness case that makes it visible
# landed in the same diff that widened kConcurrencyClaim to mention it, which is
# the ruled procedure: the wording tracks the harness and never outruns it.
